in a time for truth Texas  senator Ted Cruz talks about his journey  from a Cuban immigrant son to the US  Senate Carly Fiorina former CEO of  Hewlett Packard shares lessons she's  learned in rising to the challenge  former Arkansas governor Mike Huckabee  gives his take on politics and culture  in God Guns grits and gravy and Ohio  Governor John Kasich calls for a return  to what he sees as traditional American  values and stand for something Kentucky  Senator Rand Paul calls for smaller  government and more bipartisanship and  taking a stand more presidential  hopefuls with books include Florida  Republican Senator Marco Rubio and  American Dreams he outlines his economic  plan independent Vermont senator Bernie  Sanders has updated his 1997  autobiography  now titled outside we're in the White  House to include his time in the Senate  and the launch of his presidential  campaign in blue-collar conservatives  Rick Santorum says the Republican Party  must focus on the working class in order  to retake the White House  Donald Trump has written several  bestsellers his newest book crippled  America outlines his political platform  Carrie Johnson is the presidential  candidate for the Libertarian Party and  seven principles of good government he  discusses his political philosophy and  his time as governor of New Mexico and  finally Governor Chris Christie and  former governors Martin O'Malley and Jim  Gilmore have announced their candidacies  but haven't released books book TV has  covered many of these candidates and you  can watch them on our web site book TV  org  and now on to tonight's guest  Roger Lowenstein has reported for The  Wall Street Journal for more than a  decade including for its stock market  column heard on the street and the  intrinsic value column his work has  appeared in publications ranging from  the New York Times magazine to Bloomberg  currently Loewenstein maintains a column  for the magazine smart money he has  written several books including the  bestseller buffett origins of the crash  the end of Wall Street and tonight's  topic of discussion America's Bank and I  do want to let you know he let me know  that there's a great review of it coming  out in the Washington Post tomorrow so  we can read about that tomorrow yes  three children and lives with his wife  in Newton Massachusetts so please join  me in welcoming Roger Lowenstein  [Applause]  Debra thanks very much and it was a  wonderful  maiden introduction I'm glad I don't  have to talk about dinosaurs because I  don't know much about him I'll leave  that to the the next speaker what I'm  gonna do tonight is mostly talk to you  but I'm gonna read a few passages as  well that I hope conveys some of them  what I felt was the drama of the story  those of you who followed some of my  career might know that this was my first  history and for me it was really the  most exciting project I've ever been  involved in because the the people that  I wrote about reminded me so much of  people on the scene today and the book  is full of controversies that feel like  they left off the front pages of today  in fact just last night anyone who saw  the Republican debate may have seen the  Ted Cruz you know came out against the  Federal Reserve and advocated of the  system of going back to goal based money  and presumably not having a Federal  Reserve tonight I'd like to tell you  what it was like when we actually had a  system like that back 100 years ago but  before I get to that I want to start  with something more simple and basic  which is what is a Federal Reserve and  and why do we have it today if you think  about an ordinary bank you know they  they serve two functions which are kind  of self-evident for people who don't  have enough money they're places they  can go and borrow and for people who  have surplus they can go and park it and  earn interest but besides helping out  those two groups of people they serve a  larger social function which is there a  way of getting money that would  otherwise just sit there in the you know  wherever rich people keep their extra  money in their basements or you know in  some safe and their yachts or something  and and move it to someplace where it's  useful to society where where to people  who actually have a need for it so it's  banks fulfill that social function a  central bank is just a banker to banks  just as some people have access funds  some banks have access funds they may  have a few extra billion dollars  flashing around  and they can park their excess reserves  in any one of a network of reserve banks  around the country which is what the  Federal Reserve is and obviously just  like some people are in need of funds  sometimes banks run out of money and  they can borrow from the Federal Reserve  and we saw that in a big way during the  financial crisis in 2008 and 2009 when  the Fed became the the classic role that  it was assigned for the lender of last  resort it's really important in a crisis  because what happens in a crisis is it's  not only the the sick banks that stopped  lending but the healthy banks when they  see other banks in trouble they say I'm  gonna pull back I better stop lending  and you get this this dynamic whereby  every bank does what it has to do to  protect itself to call in loans but  they're all doing something that hurts  the community at large that is to say  depriving the country of credit and the  the role of the central bank is to  so-called lien against the wind and lend  when when no one else is after the  mortgage crisis my editor suggested to  me that it might be interesting and fun  to look at what the country was like  before we had Federal Reserve and and  how we got one  so I plunged into archives and as I said  this is the first time that I've done  primarily or in this place exclusively  archival research and that's a really  different world from reporting there  were lots of things I couldn't do I  couldn't call up Woodrow Wilson and ask  him you know why he'd done such and such  and and so on it would have been a not a  very lively conversation but but I  discovered that there are things that an  asourian can do that or things get  revealed to a Nestorian that that might  not to a contemporaneous researcher the  the Federal Reserve Act the the peak of  the legislative process was in the  summer of 1913 Washington then like now  Washington DC was a very hot place to  live and work and Ellen Wilson the  president's rather delicate wife left  town for New Hampshire during the summer  which wasn't uncommon  for political spouses in that era and so  since she was in another town we have  the record day by day of what Wilson was  writing to her which committees in  Congress were giving him trouble which  congressman he was leaning on who who in  his cabinet was giving him trouble you  know today you wouldn't have that the  the first couple would be in the same  city or they'd be you know talking on  the telephone and so on in other ways  the ear of felt completely similar to  now it was a period of great financial  stress there was a financial crisis  people were very angry at the banks many  the banks misbehaved they were angry at  the government Ted Cruz has nothing on  the populace of the late 19th and early  20th century that they in that day and  age they were Democrats now they're you  know when the Tea Party wing of the  Republican Party but the feelings are  largely the same the people didn't like  big banks they didn't like Washington  and they didn't want Washington anywhere  near a big bank itself and I'm going to  tell the this story through the eyes of  one particular character who had to  confront these feelings and I say  confront because he was not an American  his name was Paul Warburg and he found  the attitudes of his adopted country  very bizarre to say the least but  Warburg would play a very big role in  the in the formation even though he was  not an American of the Federal Reserve  he would later write in his memoirs that  his hope was that the Federal Reserve  would become a great National Monument  as he put it rather poetically like the  great cathedrals of Europe and his life  was really one long struggle to try to  make that so he was born into a very  wealthy banking family in Hamburg in the  second half of the 19th century he had  the three or four brothers who were  involved in the family banking business  with him but he quickly became the heir  apparent and by his late 20s was a set  to run the family bank however that  point he fell  with the daughter of an American banking  family Nina lobe of the Kuhn Loeb  banking family they got married in  around 1899 1900 and Paul and Nina at  first settled in Germany but she wasn't  too happy living away from her folks and  long story short they relocated to to  New York very quickly after Warburg got  to New York he realized how different  the system in his adopted country was in  Europe all the banks had their reserves  in this central reserve in in America  each bank kept its own reserves  I say reserves I mean rainy day money  extra money and when I say kept their  own that keep them in their vault or  they might Park them in a bigger Bank  that paid a little higher interest down  the block so a bank in Tacoma might have  kept some of its reserves in a bank here  in Seattle and a bank in Seattle might  have then kept its reserves in a bank in  San Francisco but whenever there was  tightness this whole system would back  up the bank in in Tacoma would call in  its money from Seattle Seattle would  send a wire to San Francisco and you'd  get this money tightness and sometimes a  panic throughout the area and sometimes  throughout the country but believe it or  not there was this would happen to some  extent every fall when the harvest  season came in every farming community  and back then farming communities you  know held more than half the population  United States they were a very big part  of the economy the farming communities  would need cash to fund the harvest to  pay field hands  finance equipment grain houses all of  that cash would drain out of the cities  and there would be a mini panic rates in  New York would go up to a hundred  percent sometimes and Paul Warburg saw  this very soon after he emigrated to the  United States and he was absolutely  astonished and I want to take you now to  to an episode that shows his reaction  right after he moved to New York  Warburg was shocked by the primitiveness  of American Finance whereas banks in  Germany functioned with near military  cohesiveness banking in America he  concluded suffered from an ethos of  extreme individualism as credit  tightened each bank pulled its loans  thereby accentuating the scarcity for  all the rest in 1903  soon after a plunge on Wall Street  Warburg led on to Jacob Schiff his  brother-in-law and the senior partner at  Kuhn Loeb Warburg firm that he had  penned some thoughts on the defects of  American banking and how to cure them  the key problem that Warburg outlined  was the lack of a central reserve Schiff  read Warburg's paper and agreed with the  substance of it  however shift said Warburg had misread  the psychology the American people who  would never accept any institution  resembling a central bank he warned his  junior partner not to share his paper  with others but as a teaching exercise  for protege he offered to show the paper  on a confidential basis to a well-placed  friend James a Stillman who is president  of the National City Bank forerunner of  today's citi bank and one of the  prominent bankers in New York a day or  two later  Warburg looked up from his desk and  found Stillman looming over him Stillman  was a man of legendary licinus ism  highly eccentric he was silent now  looking at Warburg who half-closed heavy  eyes finally adopting a tone of gentle  sarcasm Stillman spoke how was the great  international financier he added  somewhat defensively Warburg don't you  think Citibank has done pretty well  Warburg agreed that it had very well  indeed why not then leave things well  enough alone  Warburg hesitated to reply he knew how  powerful mr. Stillman was finally though  he said your bank is so big and so  powerful mr. Stillman that when the next  panic comes you will wish your  responsibilities were smaller Stillman  went off in a huff the Warburg went back  to his work Warburg had stumbled on what  was really the original America
 

